; str-eq-absence-band.fk — what equality answers when one side is the axiom-1 absence.
;
; WHY THIS BAND EXISTS. A comparison is a question ABOUT two values; a length is a MEASUREMENT
; of one. str_len says so out loud — "nothing has no length, ask nothing? before measuring" —
; and that refusal is right. str_eq is the other kind, and on 2026-09-04 the four kernels did
; not agree about it: fkwu observed the absence and answered, while go, rust and typescript all
; refused their argument door. model-discovery.fk found it, because a walk over a live directory
; is the ordinary way a body is handed a file that has left, and one kernel answering a question
; the other three die on is a divergence, not a difference in strictness.
;
; WHAT IS PINNED HERE, and why each line is its own bit. The contract was PROBED on fkwu before
; it was written anywhere (never invented, then taught to the others): an absence equals an
; absence and equals nothing else. Bit 4 is the one that matters most and the one an
; "absence is falsy, so treat it as empty" repair would break: `nothing` is NOT "". That is the
; emptymask distinction — a file that never was against a file that is there holding no bytes —
; and equality is where a careless heal would quietly collapse it. Bits 16 and 32 keep ordinary
; strings in the band so a kernel cannot pass by answering 0 to everything.
;
; Verdict 63 = 1 absence-eq-absence | 2 absence-ne-string | 4 absence-ne-empty
;            | 8 empty-ne-absence (asked from both sides — an equality that is not symmetric
;              is a worse answer than a refusal) | 16 strings-still-equal | 32 strings-still-differ
;
; preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk

(do
    (defn sab-bit (ok bit) (if ok bit 0))
    (add (sab-bit (eq (str_eq (nothing) (nothing)) 1) 1)
    (add (sab-bit (eq (str_eq (nothing) "x") 0) 2)
    (add (sab-bit (eq (str_eq (nothing) "") 0) 4)
    (add (sab-bit (eq (str_eq "" (nothing)) 0) 8)
    (add (sab-bit (eq (str_eq "same" "same") 1) 16)
         (sab-bit (eq (str_eq "same" "other") 0) 32)))))))
